Under `--modular-termination`, a field, collection or generic container can now hold a bounded callable whose function reads the heap, `bounded<O, A ~> B>`: its formation checks a lambda's body in every later state, and each application checks the bound of the value it reads. A parameter with a `decreases {:callback}` bound can be certified with `bounded.certify` and kept, and a lambda can apply it or pass it on, when the parameter's bound on entry is below the bound the lambda runs under. So an observer, an iterator or a stored callback can keep a call budget that reads mutable state and spend it later. Without the option, nothing changes. See https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/266.
